## New findings

### DP-r2-01 — MAJOR — D0 NULL enforcement is not designed

The proposal says NULL is forbidden, but specifies only:

- nullable columns retro-added to existing tables;
- a unique index;
- expanded shared INSERT statements;
- an unnamed “CHECK-equivalent enforcement at the single insert statement.”

A unique index does not prohibit NULL coordinate pairs, and an INSERT with coordinate placeholders still accepts explicit NULLs. It also cannot protect out-of-band INSERTs unless the table has a real `CHECK`, `NOT NULL`, or trigger.

This contradicts both “enforced, not audited-for” and G-D0-5’s required NULL refusal ([design-proposal.md:119](/Users/kaygee/Code/loops/docs/scratch/arrival-sliceD/design-proposal.md:119), [design-proposal.md:465](/Users/kaygee/Code/loops/docs/scratch/arrival-sliceD/design-proposal.md:465)).

Required change: name an actual database invariant—most cleanly rebuilding each table with `NOT NULL` plus `UNIQUE`, or adding explicit `CHECK`/trigger enforcement that covers every writer, including foreign SQL.

### DP-r2-02 — MAJOR — `coordinate_axis` migration dispatch does not cover ordinary legacy opens

The document assigns migration to `arrival_projection._ensure_index_schema` and says it runs “before the connection is handed out” ([design-proposal.md:150](/Users/kaygee/Code/loops/docs/scratch/arrival-sliceD/design-proposal.md:150)). In current source, that helper belongs to projection rederivation and is called there ([arrival_projection.py:479](/Users/kaygee/Code/loops/libs/engine/src/engine/arrival_projection.py:479), [arrival_projection.py:544](/Users/kaygee/Code/loops/libs/engine/src/engine/arrival_projection.py:544)); ordinary `SqliteStore` and `JsonlStore` opens do not route through it.

Yet changing global `FACT_INSERT_SQL`/`TICK_INSERT_SQL` affects those ordinary writers immediately ([sqlite_store.py:131](/Users/kaygee/Code/loops/libs/engine/src/engine/sqlite_store.py:131), [jsonl_store.py:668](/Users/kaygee/Code/loops/libs/engine/src/engine/jsonl_store.py:668)). An existing legacy database can therefore encounter new-column SQL before any stated migration path has installed/backfilled those columns.

Required change: define a common schema upgrader invoked by every writable store constructor before any insert SQL is used, with mode passed explicitly or derived authoritatively. Gate existing sqlite-canonical and jsonl-canonical databases opened through their normal public APIs.

### DP-r2-03 — MAJOR — D4 `key_registry` extraction conflicts with exact-admission verification unless selectively defined

The proposed registry is described as a refactor of the registry that `verify_authorship` already constructs ([design-proposal.md:405](/Users/kaygee/Code/loops/docs/scratch/arrival-sliceD/design-proposal.md:405)). But current `verify_authorship` verifies every signed arrival envelope while walking the source ([arrival.py:1597](/Users/kaygee/Code/loops/libs/engine/src/engine/arrival.py:1597), [arrival.py:1627](/Users/kaygee/Code/loops/libs/engine/src/engine/arrival.py:1627)). G-D4-2 simultaneously requires a bad envelope on a deduplicated row not to refuse admission ([design-proposal.md:510](/Users/kaygee/Code/loops/docs/scratch/arrival-sliceD/design-proposal.md:510)).

Those properties coexist only if `key_registry`:

- structurally walks the whole log;
- verifies genesis and key-introduction envelopes required to establish registry authority;
- does not verify unrelated row-record envelopes;
- records introduction ordinals so row signatures are checked only against keys introduced before the row’s source ordinal.

The proposal states the final temporal rule but does not define this selective extraction behavior. A straightforward refactor of the existing loop fails G-D4-2; skipping all envelope verification would admit unverified key introductions. Source confirms key introduction is authorized only after its own signature verifies under a previously valid key ([arrival.py:1627](/Users/kaygee/Code/loops/libs/engine/src/engine/arrival.py:1627), [arrival.py:1648](/Users/kaygee/Code/loops/libs/engine/src/engine/arrival.py:1648)).

Required change: specify the selective registry algorithm and add a forged key-introduction gate in addition to the deduplicated ordinary-envelope case.

### DP-r2-04 — minor — G-D3-2 offers timing as an invalid correctness oracle

G-D3-2 permits “wall-time flat in store size” as a proxy for O(1) record verification ([design-proposal.md:500](/Users/kaygee/Code/loops/docs/scratch/arrival-sliceD/design-proposal.md:500)). Timing is nondeterministic and cannot prove which substrate methods or records were consumed.

The design already identifies the correct alternative: instrument record-verification calls. Make that mandatory and assert exact/bounded call counts; do not permit timing as the gate oracle.

## Per-decision verdict

| Decision | Verdict | Reason |
|---|---|---|
| D0 | UNSOUND | The mode-aware coordinate derivation is correct, but NULL enforcement and migration dispatch across ordinary legacy store opens remain unrealized. |
| D1 | SOUND-WITH-CHANGES | The witness model, app routing, and typed axis mismatch are coherent, contingent on D0 becoming a dependable invariant. |
| D2 | SOUND-WITH-CHANGES | Construction and verification are now comprehensively re-keyed; correctness depends on repairing D0 migration/enforcement. |
| D3 | SOUND-WITH-CHANGES | The anchor verb, rewound check, and multiset audit are sound. Replace the timing proxy with deterministic instrumentation. |
| D4 | SOUND-WITH-CHANGES | Post-dedup fact-commitment verification is the correct design, but registry extraction must selectively verify registry-forming records without reinstating whole-log envelope admission checks. |
| Gate plan | UNSOUND | It contains an unenforceable-as-specified D0 assertion, omits normal legacy-open migration gates, lacks forged key-introduction coverage, and permits a timing oracle. |

Overall: **NOT-RATIFIABLE-AS-WRITTEN**.
